    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d369">

                <pb facs="0581" n="535" xml:id="pg535"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">812.00/15127½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Wilson to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-06-02">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">2 June, 1915</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: I like those suggestions
                    very much indeed, and hope that when the proper time comes you will act on
                    them.</p>
                <p>Faithfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. W.</signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>P. S. It is very interesting that Lansing should have made the same
                        suggestion about the closing words of the statement. It furnished me with
                        exactly what I was looking for.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>W. W.</signed>
                    </closer>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
